WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:36.800
Speaker 2: Hi, David, how are you doing? I'm&nbsp;
doing well. So I wanted to touch on this topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.040
given the Democratic leadership you had Steny&nbsp;
Hoyer and Jim Clyburn. And there tends to be this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.120
conventional wisdom that progressive Democrats&nbsp;
can only win in districts like a on a Pressley's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.920
and that if we were to turn the Democratic Party&nbsp;
toward a more progressive direction that we would,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:02.480
that we would lose nationwide. And there is a&nbsp;
piece on Jacobin about this, given the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.400
rural voters have really turned toward the&nbsp;
Republican Party. And the reality is that in 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.360
I have seen a candidate that has run&nbsp;
a totally grassroots, people powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.640
congressional bid. And that's Derrick Marshall.&nbsp;
He's running in the 23rd Congressional District,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.480
down in the high desert of Southern California.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.760
And I was just curious if you've been keeping&nbsp;
tabs on the midterms and if there's any.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.360
That had, like struck your eye as candidates&nbsp;
running in rural areas, places that are maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.760
unconventional so that we can continue to fight&nbsp;
for issues that are affecting people who are maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.240
most disproportionately affected by&nbsp;
poverty and by the day to day struggle.&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:52.640
Speaker 1: I can't say specifically that there's&nbsp;
progressive rural candidates that I would be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:58.080
to sort of like recommend to anybody. But my&nbsp;
recommendation would be if you are in a rural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:04.080
area, find out who's running because my capacity&nbsp;
on the show to know about every candidate running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.800
everywhere is is very limited in my ability to&nbsp;
interview all of them is also very limited. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.880
we're going to have very soon. I was interviewed&nbsp;
by Farron Cousins in Las Vegas recently, and he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.920
going to be publishing an interview. We did sort&nbsp;
of about exactly this issue, which is how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.320
deal with figuring out what races to get involved&nbsp;
in? And I'm going to be talking about it on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:30.080
show as well. But but you know, and unfortunately,&nbsp;
it's beyond the scope of my show to tell everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.920
who to support, et cetera, et cetera. But there's&nbsp;
no shortage of great candidates out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.320
and it's great that you're paying attention.
Speaker 2: I'm thinking,&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.680
Speaker 1: all right there. There we go.&nbsp;
Very, very important message for sure.

